# 👥 Customer Segmentation with RFM Analysis (Python)

**Goal:** Segment customers into meaningful groups using Recency, Frequency, and Monetary value so marketing can target them better.

**Stack:** Python · Pandas · NumPy · Matplotlib · Seaborn

---

## 📌 Business Question

Which customers are our most valuable, which are at risk of churning, and which are new/promising?

---

## 📂 Project Structure

```
02-python-customer-segmentation/
├── data/
│   ├── raw/
│   └── cleaned/
├── notebooks/
│   └── rfm_analysis.ipynb          (main analysis)
├── scripts/
│   └── rfm_pipeline.py             (reusable script)
├── outputs/
│   ├── segments_summary.csv
│   └── charts/
└── README.md
```

---

## 🔍 Method

1. Calculate **Recency** (days since last purchase)
2. Calculate **Frequency** (number of orders)
3. Calculate **Monetary** (total spend)
4. Score each customer 1–5 on R, F, M
5. Combine scores into segments:
   - Champions
   - Loyal Customers
   - Potential Loyalists
   - At Risk
   - Hibernating
   - New Customers

---

## 📊 Key Findings (example – replace with yours)

| Segment          | % of Customers | % of Revenue | Action                          |
|------------------|----------------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| Champions        | 12%            | 45%          | Reward & upsell                 |
| Loyal            | 18%            | 25%          | Loyalty program                 |
| At Risk          | 15%            | 12%          | Win-back campaign               |
| Hibernating      | 22%            | 5%           | Re-activation or drop           |

---

## ▶️ How to run

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
jupyter notebook notebooks/rfm_analysis.ipynb
# or
python scripts/rfm_pipeline.py
```

---

## 💡 Recommendation

Focus retention budget on “At Risk” and “Loyal” segments. Champions should get exclusive offers to increase average order value.